---
/* WHAT YOU HAVE ALREADY SEARCHED, KEPT PAGE BY PAGE.

   David: «i want to be able to store the page im up to, or pages ive done,
   not just a yes/no/partial» — and, on what this atlas is for: «this was
   always a geneology tool, now it just isnt a info tool it actually give
   something».

   RANGES, BECAUSE THAT IS HOW THE WORK IS ACTUALLY DONE. Nobody searches a
   register and comes away with a boolean. They come away with «I did images
   1 to 47, then the light went». So the unit stored is a set of ranges, the
   input accepts what a person would write — «1-47, 60-72» — and the thing
   the page shows back is where to start again.

   NO INVENTED DENOMINATOR. This atlas does NOT know how long a volume is: a
   volume row carries a waypoint, a title and years, and the only image counts
   in the data are collection-level aggregates like 283,815,879 for Find a
   Grave. So there is no progress bar until the reader types the total they
   can see on the film themselves, and when they do it is labelled as theirs.
   A percentage this map made up would be the one thing it exists not to do.

   LOCALSTORAGE, AND THIS TIME THAT IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. Elsewhere in
   this project localStorage is used for conveniences, with the note that it
   «is a convenience and not a record». Page ranges ARE a record — somebody
   may spend months building them — and site data gets cleared, private
   windows forget, browsers evict under pressure, laptops get replaced. So
   export is a first-class verb here rather than a footnote, and the pages
   that show progress say plainly where it lives.

   THE ROW SHAPE BORROWS FROM THE ARCHIVE KIT, which already had to answer
   «what is being done» for eight family archives: `covers` is a list of
   stable ids, `since` is when it was raised. Keeping the same two words means
   a reader's own progress can be exported into an archive's worklist.json
   instead of being a second, incompatible record of the same work. `owner`
   and `state` are dropped: there is one owner here, and a range says more
   than a state does.

   ONE VOLUME, SEVERAL FAMILIES, DIFFERENT WORK.

   David: «if I'm researching multiple families… searching for Blažević in
   Senj may be different progress than if I am doing Kosina in Senj.»

   He is right, and his own archives prove it: inside the Blažević archive,
   Kranj–Šmartin marriages 1780–1815 was worked for KOSINAR and 1816–1855 for
   KOSINA. Same parish, same shelf, different line. Across all 1,349 rows in
   the eight archives, 519 describe hunting a named family and 72 describe
   walking a book with no name in sight.

   BECAUSE THOSE ARE TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF WORK, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM
   GENERALISES.
     · WALKING a register — reading every entry — is true for every family
       ever after. Pages 1–174 walked are walked.
     · SCANNING for a name is true for that name and nothing else. «1–47
       scanned for Blažević» says nothing whatever about Kosina.
   Version 1 stored one `done` per volume and quietly treated the second as
   the first, which overstates the work on every line but the one it was
   done for — and the entire value of this record is knowing what is still
   left to do.

   So a mark now carries `walked`, shared by everyone, and `by[line]`, which
   is not. What a reader sees for a line is the union of the two.

   MIGRATION CLAIMS NOTHING. Marks made under v1 have no line and were mostly
   made while hunting one family, so promoting them to `walked` would assert
   coverage for families that were never looked for. They go into a single
   line instead, named «My research so far», which is renameable — David
   chose this over the promotion when asked. Nothing is lost and nothing is
   claimed.
*/
---
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    /* THE KEY DID NOT CHANGE. A reader's v1 record is read, migrated in
       place and written back under the same key — a new key would have left
       months of work sitting in storage that nothing reads any more, which
       is the loss this whole file exists to prevent. */
    var KEY = "ra-progress-v1";
    var mem = null;

    /* The line every pre-line mark lands in. Renameable, and named as a
       statement of fact rather than as a family, because it is not one. */
    var FIRST_LINE = "my-research-so-far";

    function slugify(s) {
      return String(s || "").toLowerCase()
        .normalize ? String(s || "").toLowerCase().normalize("NFKD")
          .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, "").replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-")
          .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, "").slice(0, 48)
        : String(s || "").toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-");
    }

    function load() {
      if (mem) return mem;
      mem = { v: 1, marks: {} };
      try {
        var raw = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
        if (raw) {
          var d = JSON.parse(raw);
          if (d && d.marks) mem = d;
        }
      } catch (e) { /* private window, blocked storage — carry on empty */ }
      /* MARKS MADE BEFORE THE KEYS AGREED ARE MOVED, NOT DROPPED. The place
         page shipped keying volumes by their image URL; the map panel keys
         them by waypoint. Anyone who marked a volume in that window has it
         under the old key, and canonical() would simply stop finding it —
         which is exactly the silent loss of somebody's work this whole file
         is written to avoid. Migrated once, on read, and the result is saved
         so it happens once rather than every load. */
      var moved = 0;
      Object.keys(mem.marks).forEach(function (k) {
        var c = canonical(k);
        if (c === k) return;
        var from = mem.marks[k];
        var into = mem.marks[c];
        if (!into) { mem.marks[c] = from; }
        else {
          /* Both keys used: union the ranges rather than pick a winner. */
          into.done = merge((into.done || []).concat(from.done || []));
          if (from.total && !into.total) into.total = from.total;
          if (from.note && from.note !== into.note) {
            into.note = into.note ? into.note + " \u00b7 " + from.note : from.note;
          }
          if (from.t && !into.t) into.t = from.t;
          if (from.where && !into.where) into.where = from.where;
          if (from.href && !into.href) into.href = from.href;
        }
        delete mem.marks[k];
        moved++;
      });
      if (moved) { try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(mem)); } catch (e) {} }
      if (toLines()) { try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(mem)); } catch (e) {} }
      return mem;
    }

    /* v1 -> v2, ONCE, ON READ. A v1 mark carried `done`, `state`, `note` and
       `since` at its top level and no idea which family they were for. They
       move wholesale into one line rather than into `walked`: see the header.
       Idempotent, because a record that has already been through this has a
       `lines` map and is left alone. */
    function toLines() {
      if (mem.lines && mem.v >= 2) return false;
      mem.lines = mem.lines || {};
      mem.v = 2;
      var any = false;
      Object.keys(mem.marks).forEach(function (k) {
        var m = mem.marks[k];
        if (m.by) return;
        var mine = {};
        ["done", "state", "note", "since"].forEach(function (f) {
          if (m[f] !== undefined) { mine[f] = m[f]; delete m[f]; }
        });
        m.by = {};
        if (Object.keys(mine).length) { m.by[FIRST_LINE] = mine; any = true; }
      });
      if (any && !mem.lines[FIRST_LINE]) {
        mem.lines[FIRST_LINE] = { name: "My research so far", where: "",
                                  made: today() };
      }
      if (!Object.keys(mem.lines).length) {
        mem.lines[FIRST_LINE] = { name: "My research so far", where: "",
                                  made: today() };
      }
      if (!mem.active || !mem.lines[mem.active]) {
        mem.active = Object.keys(mem.lines)[0];
      }
      return true;
    }

    function activeSlug() {
      var d = load();
      if (!d.active || !d.lines[d.active]) d.active = Object.keys(d.lines)[0];
      return d.active;
    }

    /* WHAT A LINE CAN SEE: everything walked, plus what was scanned for it.
       Walked pages are shared because reading every entry on a page is a
       fact about the page, not about who was being looked for. */
    function coverage(m, slug) {
      if (!m) return [];
      var mine = (m.by && m.by[slug] && m.by[slug].done) || [];
      return merge((m.walked || []).concat(mine));
    }

    /* The shape every existing caller already expects — `done`, `state`,
       `note`, `since` at the top — assembled for one line. Keeping this the
       return type of get() is why the map panel, the place pages and the
       rings did not each need teaching about lines. */
    function view(m, slug) {
      if (!m) return null;
      var mine = (m.by && m.by[slug]) || {};
      var cov = coverage(m, slug);
      var out = { t: m.t, where: m.where, href: m.href, total: m.total,
                  walked: m.walked || null,
                  state: mine.state, note: mine.note, since: mine.since };
      if (cov.length) out.done = cov;
      return out;
    }

    function markFor(id) {
      var m = load().marks;
      m[id] = m[id] || {};
      m[id].by = m[id].by || {};
      return m[id];
    }
    function mineFor(id, slug) {
      var m = markFor(id);
      m.by[slug] = m.by[slug] || {};
      return m.by[slug];
    }
    /* A mark nothing holds up any more is removed rather than left as an
       empty husk that still counts in «12 volumes marked». */
    function tidy(id) {
      var m = load().marks[id];
      if (!m) return;
      var lines = Object.keys(m.by || {});
      lines.forEach(function (s) {
        var b = m.by[s];
        if (!(b.done && b.done.length) && !b.state && !b.note) delete m.by[s];
      });
      if (!Object.keys(m.by || {}).length && !(m.walked || []).length) {
        delete load().marks[id];
      }
    }
    function save() {
      try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(mem)); }
      catch (e) { /* quota or blocked; the page must not break over it */ }
    }
    function today() { return new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10); }

    /* ONE BOOK, ONE KEY, WHEREVER IT IS PRINTED.
       The same register is listed on three surfaces and each had a different
       idea of its id: the map panel had a FamilySearch waypoint («9RK1-T3F»),
       the place page had none and fell back to the whole image URL, and the
       «Start here» list fell back to the title. Marking Births 1734-1756 on
       the map and opening the place page showed it unmarked — I had claimed
       these were the same id and the overlap was literally zero.
       The URL carries the waypoint inside it as `wc=`, so every form can be
       reduced to the same thing. Taken before the first colon because the
       source data disagrees with itself even there: some rows carry
       «9RK1-T3F» and others «3JXZ-K68:172062901,172062902», and the leading
       code is what they share.
       Normalised HERE rather than in the markup, so a list added later cannot
       start a fourth tally by guessing differently. */
    function canonical(id) {
      var raw = String(id || "");
      var kind = raw.indexOf(":") > -1 ? raw.slice(0, raw.indexOf(":")) : "";
      var rest = kind ? raw.slice(kind.length + 1) : raw;
      if (kind !== "volume") return raw;
      var wp = null;
      /* `owc=` AS WELL AS `wc=`. FamilySearch writes the waypoint as `wc=`
         on an ark URL and as `owc=` on a search/image one, and
         data/fs-volumes-world.json — 1,576 places — is entirely the second
         form. Matching only `wc=` meant every one of those volumes fell
         through to «a title, nothing better» and kept its own tally, which
         is the same silent split this function was written to close. */
      var m = rest.match(/[?&]o?wc=([^&]+)/);
      if (m) {
        try { wp = decodeURIComponent(m[1]); } catch (e) { wp = m[1]; }
      } else if (!/^https?:/.test(rest)) {
        wp = rest;
      }
      if (!wp) return raw;                       // a title, nothing better
      wp = wp.split(":")[0].trim();
      return wp ? "volume:" + wp : raw;
    }

    /* «1-47, 60-72» · «1–47» · «5» · «12 to 30» — whatever a person writes.
       Returns merged, sorted, non-overlapping ranges, or null if nothing in
       the string was a number. Silence beats a wrong guess: a string this
       cannot read leaves the stored ranges alone. */
    function parseRanges(text) {
      var s = String(text == null ? "" : text);
      var out = [], any = false;
      s.split(/[,;]+/).forEach(function (part) {
        var m = part.match(/(\d+)\s*(?:[-–—]|to)\s*(\d+)/);
        if (m) { any = true; out.push([+m[1], +m[2]]); return; }
        var one = part.match(/(\d+)/);
        if (one) { any = true; out.push([+one[1], +one[1]]); }
      });
      if (!any) return null;
      return merge(out);
    }
    function merge(list) {
      var r = list.map(function (p) {
        return p[0] <= p[1] ? [p[0], p[1]] : [p[1], p[0]];
      }).filter(function (p) { return p[0] >= 0; })
        .sort(function (a, b) { return a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]; });
      var out = [];
      r.forEach(function (p) {
        var last = out[out.length - 1];
        /* Adjacent ranges join: 1-47 and 48-60 is 1-60, because a reader who
           typed them separately did not mean to leave a gap of nothing. */
        if (last && p[0] <= last[1] + 1) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], p[1]);
        else out.push([p[0], p[1]]);
      });
      return out;
    }
    function fmt(ranges) {
      return (ranges || []).map(function (p) {
        return p[0] === p[1] ? String(p[0]) : p[0] + "–" + p[1];
      }).join(", ");
    }
    /* WHERE TO START AGAIN IS THE FIRST GAP, NOT THE HIGHEST PAGE. After
       1-47 and 60-72 the answer is 48: the reader skipped 48-59 and will
       want to know it. Reporting 73 would quietly bless the hole. */
    function resume(ranges) {
      if (!ranges || !ranges.length) return 1;
      if (ranges[0][0] > 1) return 1;
      return ranges[0][1] + 1;
    }
    function covered(ranges) {
      return (ranges || []).reduce(function (a, p) { return a + (p[1] - p[0] + 1); }, 0);
    }

    /* ---- THE FAMILY SESSIONS' OWN WORK, FOLDED IN ON LOAD ---------------
       David: «is there any way the my research area can just include these
       sessions in the dropdown rather, so I can add to their work and see it
       clearer?» It can, and the only reason it did not was that the file
       existed in the repository and not on the site. build.py publishes it now
       and this reads it, so the Blažević, Defranceski, Falco, Lerena, Booyzen,
       D'Arcy, Mazza and Luwinski sessions' families appear in the switcher
       without anybody running a script or importing anything.

       MERGED, NEVER IMPOSED. It goes through the same fromJSON("merge") path a
       hand import uses, which is additive by design: it adds a family that is
       not there, unions page ranges per line, and never overwrites a value
       this browser already holds. Your own marks always win.

       ONCE PER VERSION, NOT ONCE PER VISIT. The file's `exported` stamp is
       remembered; while it is unchanged nothing is merged again. Without that,
       a family deleted here would reappear on the next page load, which is the
       kind of small betrayal that makes somebody stop trusting a tool. */
    function foldInSessions() {
      var base = (document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-base") || "/");
      var url = base.replace(/\/?$/, "/") + "research-sessions.json";
      fetch(url).then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : null; })
        .then(function (doc) {
          if (!doc || !doc.marks) return;
          var stamp = "ra-sessions-seen";
          var seen = null;
          try { seen = localStorage.getItem(stamp); } catch (e) { return; }
          var v = String(doc.exported || doc.built || "");
          if (seen === v) return;
          window.raProgress.fromJSON(JSON.stringify(doc), "merge");
          try { localStorage.setItem(stamp, v); } catch (e) {}
          document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("ra-progress-changed"));
        })
        .catch(function () {});
    }

    window.raProgress = {
      get: function (id) { return view(load().marks[canonical(id)], activeSlug()); },
      /* The whole mark, every line, for the pages that show the breakdown. */
      raw: function (id) { return load().marks[canonical(id)] || null; },

      /* ---- LINES ------------------------------------------------------ */
      lines: function () {
        var d = load();
        return Object.keys(d.lines).map(function (slug) {
          var n = 0;
          Object.keys(d.marks).forEach(function (k) {
            var b = d.marks[k].by && d.marks[k].by[slug];
            if (b && ((b.done && b.done.length) || b.state || b.note)) n++;
          });
          return { slug: slug, name: d.lines[slug].name,
                   where: d.lines[slug].where || "", marks: n,
                   active: slug === d.active };
        }).sort(function (a, b) { return a.name.localeCompare(b.name); });
      },
      active: activeSlug,
      setActive: function (slug) {
        var d = load();
        if (d.lines[slug]) { d.active = slug; save(); }
        return d.active;
      },
      addLine: function (name, where) {
        var d = load();
        var base = slugify(name + (where ? "-" + where : "")) || "line";
        var slug = base, n = 2;
        while (d.lines[slug]) { slug = base + "-" + (n++); }
        d.lines[slug] = { name: String(name || "Untitled").trim(),
                          where: String(where || "").trim(), made: today() };
        d.active = slug;
        save();
        return slug;
      },
      renameLine: function (slug, name, where) {
        var d = load();
        if (!d.lines[slug]) return null;
        if (name !== undefined) d.lines[slug].name = String(name).trim() || d.lines[slug].name;
        if (where !== undefined) d.lines[slug].where = String(where).trim();
        save();
        return d.lines[slug];
      },
      /* REMOVING A LINE REMOVES ITS WORK, and nothing else's. Pages walked
         stay, because they were never this line's to begin with. The caller
         is expected to have asked first — this does not ask. */
      removeLine: function (slug) {
        var d = load();
        if (!d.lines[slug] || Object.keys(d.lines).length < 2) return false;
        delete d.lines[slug];
        Object.keys(d.marks).forEach(function (k) {
          if (d.marks[k].by) delete d.marks[k].by[slug];
          tidy(k);
        });
        if (d.active === slug) d.active = Object.keys(d.lines)[0];
        save();
        return true;
      },

      /* ---- MARKS ------------------------------------------------------ */
      /* `walked` is the one flag that changes whose work this is: pages read
         entry by entry belong to every line, so they are stored once at the
         top of the mark instead of under the line that happened to be open. */
      setRanges: function (id, text, opts) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var r = parseRanges(text);
        var walked = !!(opts && opts.walked);
        var slug = activeSlug();
        var d = load();
        if (r === null) {
          if (String(text || "").trim() === "") {
            if (walked) { if (d.marks[id]) delete d.marks[id].walked; }
            else if (d.marks[id] && d.marks[id].by) delete d.marks[id].by[slug];
            tidy(id);
          }
        } else if (walked) {
          var m = markFor(id);
          m.walked = r;
          /* Pages now known to have been read in full are no longer this
             line's private business; leaving a duplicate copy under the line
             would double-count in any per-line total. */
          if (m.by[slug] && m.by[slug].done) {
            m.by[slug].done = m.by[slug].done.filter(function (p) {
              return !r.some(function (w) { return p[0] >= w[0] && p[1] <= w[1]; });
            });
            if (!m.by[slug].done.length) delete m.by[slug].done;
          }
          m.by[slug] = m.by[slug] || {};
          m.by[slug].since = today();
        } else {
          var b = mineFor(id, slug);
          b.done = r;
          b.since = today();
        }
        save();
        return view(d.marks[id], slug);
      },
      setTotal: function (id, n) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var m = markFor(id);
        n = parseInt(n, 10);
        if (n > 0) m.total = n; else delete m.total;
        mineFor(id, activeSlug()).since = today();
        save();
        return view(m, activeSlug());
      },
      setNote: function (id, s) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var b = mineFor(canonical(id), activeSlug());
        if (String(s || "").trim()) b.note = String(s).trim();
        else delete b.note;
        b.since = today();
        tidy(id);
        save();
        return view(load().marks[id], activeSlug());
      },
      /* The label is the BOOK's, not the line's, so it stays at the top of
         the mark where every line reads the same one. */
      setWhat: function (id, what) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var m = markFor(id);
        if (what && what.t) m.t = what.t;
        if (what && what.where) m.where = what.where;
        if (what && what.href) m.href = what.href;
        save();
        return view(m, activeSlug());
      },
      setState: function (id, v) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var slug = activeSlug();
        var b = mineFor(id, slug);
        if (v) { b.state = v; b.since = today(); }
        else { delete b.state; }
        tidy(id);
        save();
        return view(load().marks[id], slug);
      },
      /* Clearing clears THIS LINE. A shared walk survives it, because one
         family's researcher deciding they are done is not a statement that
         the pages were never read. */
      clear: function (id) {
        id = canonical(id);
        var d = load();
        if (d.marks[id] && d.marks[id].by) delete d.marks[id].by[activeSlug()];
        tidy(id);
        save();
      },
      all: function () { return load().marks; },
      count: function () {
        var d = load(), slug = activeSlug(), n = 0;
        Object.keys(d.marks).forEach(function (k) {
          var b = d.marks[k].by && d.marks[k].by[slug];
          if ((b && ((b.done && b.done.length) || b.state || b.note)) ||
              (d.marks[k].walked || []).length) n++;
        });
        return n;
      },
      toJSON: function () {
        var d = load();
        return JSON.stringify({ v: 2, exported: today(), lines: d.lines,
                                active: d.active, marks: d.marks }, null, 1);
      },
      fromJSON: function (text, mode) {
        var inc = JSON.parse(text);
        if (!inc || !inc.marks) throw new Error("not a Record Atlas progress file");
        var d = load();
        /* A v1 FILE STILL IMPORTS. It has no lines, so its marks land in one
           named after the file's own date — not merged into whatever line
           happens to be open, which would attribute somebody else's work to
           this family. */
        if (!inc.lines) {
          var slug = "imported-" + (inc.exported || today());
          if (!d.lines[slug]) {
            d.lines[slug] = { name: "Imported " + (inc.exported || today()),
                              where: "", made: today() };
          }
          var lifted = {};
          Object.keys(inc.marks).forEach(function (k) {
            var m = inc.marks[k], mine = {};
            ["done", "state", "note", "since"].forEach(function (f) {
              if (m[f] !== undefined) { mine[f] = m[f]; delete m[f]; }
            });
            m.by = {}; m.by[slug] = mine;
            lifted[k] = m;
          });
          inc.marks = lifted;
        } else {
          Object.keys(inc.lines).forEach(function (sl) {
            if (!d.lines[sl]) d.lines[sl] = inc.lines[sl];
          });
        }
        Object.keys(inc.marks).forEach(function (k) {
          var incoming = inc.marks[k];
          if (mode === "replace" || !d.marks[k]) { d.marks[k] = incoming; return; }
          var mine = d.marks[k];
          mine.t = mine.t || incoming.t;
          mine.where = mine.where || incoming.where;
          mine.href = mine.href || incoming.href;
          if (incoming.total && !mine.total) mine.total = incoming.total;
          if (incoming.walked) mine.walked = merge((mine.walked || []).concat(incoming.walked));
          mine.by = mine.by || {};
          Object.keys(incoming.by || {}).forEach(function (sl) {
            var a = mine.by[sl], b = incoming.by[sl];
            if (!a) { mine.by[sl] = b; return; }
            /* MERGING TWO MACHINES MUST NOT LOSE A PAGE — per line, now,
               because a union across lines would hand one family credit for
               another's reading. */
            a.done = merge((a.done || []).concat(b.done || []));
            if (!a.done.length) delete a.done;
            if (b.state && !a.state) a.state = b.state;
            if (b.note && b.note !== a.note) {
              a.note = a.note ? a.note + " \u00b7 " + b.note : b.note;
            }
            if (b.since && (!a.since || b.since > a.since)) a.since = b.since;
          });
        });
        save();
        return Object.keys(inc.marks).length;
      },
      coverage: function (id, slug) {
        return coverage(load().marks[canonical(id)], slug || activeSlug());
      },
      canonical: canonical,
      parseRanges: parseRanges,
      fmt: fmt,
      resume: resume,
      covered: covered
    };
      foldInSessions();
  })();
</script>
